// The gpu package: Cone's own thin GPU layer, shaped like WebGPU's objects,
// the common subset of Vulkan, Direct3D 12 and Metal, with Vulkan (the vulkan
// package) as its first and only backend. Nothing of Vulkan's appears in what
// it offers: a program uses these objects, and a later backend puts another
// API under the same ones, as Godot's RenderingDevice puts Vulkan, Direct3D 12
// and Metal under one interface.
//
//   mut win = Window.openVulkan("triangle");            // the window package
//   mut instance = Instance.create("triangle");
//   mut surface = instance.createSurface(win.sdlHandle());
//   mut adapter = instance.requestAdapter(&surface, POWER_PREFERENCE_HIGH_PERFORMANCE);
//   mut device = adapter.requestDevice();
//   imm size = win.size();
//   mut swapchain = device.createSwapChain(&surface, u32[size.w], u32[size.h], TEXTURE_FORMAT_UNDEFINED);
//   mut shader = device.createShaderModule(&TRIANGLE_SPIRV);   // SPIR-V, embedded
//   mut layout = device.createEmptyPipelineLayout(0u32);
//   imm attributes = [VertexAttribute[VERTEX_FORMAT_FLOAT32X2, 0u64, 0u32],
//     VertexAttribute[VERTEX_FORMAT_FLOAT32X3, 8u64, 1u32]];
//   imm buffers = [VertexBufferLayout[arrayStride: 20u64, attributes: &attributes]];
//   mut pipeline = device.createRenderPipeline(&RenderPipelineDescriptor[layout: &layout,
//     vertexModule: &shader, buffers: &buffers, fragmentModule: &shader, colorFormat: swapchain.format]);
//   mut vertices = device.createBuffer(60u64, BUFFER_USAGE_VERTEX | BUFFER_USAGE_COPY_DST);
//   device.queue.writeBuffer(&vertices, 0u64, &data as *u8, 60u64);
//   while win.running {
//     win.pollEvents();
//     imm frame = swapchain.getCurrentTexture(&mut device);
//     if frame.ok() {
//       mut encoder = device.createCommandEncoder();
//       imm attachment = RenderPassColorAttachment[view: frame.texture.createView(),
//         clearValue: Color[0.1, 0.2, 0.4, 1.]];
//       mut pass = encoder.beginRenderPass(&attachment);
//       pass.setPipeline(&pipeline);
//       pass.setVertexBuffer(0u32, &vertices, 0u64);
//       pass.draw(3u32, 1u32, 0u32, 0u32);
//       pass.end();
//       device.queue.submit(encoder.finish());
//       swapchain.present(&mut device);
//     }
//   }
//
// The objects are WebGPU's: an Instance finds Adapters (GPUs), an Adapter
// makes a Device, which has a Queue; a Surface is a window's, and a SwapChain
// hands out its Textures to draw into, one a frame. A Device makes Buffers,
// Textures (to draw into, or for shaders to sample, filled by
// 'queue.writeTexture'), Samplers, ShaderModules (from SPIR-V),
// BindGroupLayouts, PipelineLayouts, RenderPipelines and BindGroups (of
// buffers, textures and samplers). Commands are recorded with a
// CommandEncoder, drawing inside a RenderPass that clears or keeps each
// attachment, and finished into a CommandBuffer the Queue submits. Where
// WebGPU's shape and Vulkan's differ, this follows WebGPU's names and says so:
//
// - The swapchain is its own object, made by the Device from a Surface
//   (WebGPU's 'device.createSwapChain', before 2023 moved it onto the
//   surface), so that it is made after the device and ends before it: each
//   object here is ended by its finalizer, in the reverse of the order it was
//   declared, which is the order Vulkan's objects must be destroyed in. It
//   also makes the depth texture drawing with depth needs, at its own size,
//   made again with it.
// - 'getCurrentTexture' and 'present' are handed the Device, which owns the
//   frames in flight their synchronization belongs to.
// - A render pipeline's descriptor is one flat struct, WebGPU's 'primitive',
//   'depthStencil' and 'fragment' members' fields in it, since a Cone field
//   cannot default to a struct literal; it has one color target.
// - Push constants are WebGPU's "immediates": a pipeline layout's
//   'immediateSize' bytes (at most MAX_IMMEDIATE_SIZE, 128), seen by the
//   vertex and the fragment stage, set by 'pass.setImmediates'.
//
// Coordinates are WebGPU's, whatever the backend's: clip space has y up and
// depth from 0 to 1, and a texture's first row is its top. On Vulkan, whose
// clip space has y down, the viewport is flipped (a negative height, as wgpu
// and most engines do), so a triangle counterclockwise in clip space is
// counterclockwise on the screen, and FRONT_FACE_CCW means what it says.
//
// Frames in flight. The CPU records frame N+1 while the GPU draws frame N:
// there are FRAMES_IN_FLIGHT (2) sets of per-frame state, each a fence the
// frame's last submission signals, the command pools its commands are
// recorded from, the staging memory its uploads are copied from, and the
// semaphore its swapchain image is acquired with. A frame begins
// (implicitly, with its first 'getCurrentTexture', 'createCommandEncoder',
// 'writeBuffer' or 'createTexture') by waiting for the fence of the frame two
// back and resetting that frame's pools and staging memory, so nothing still
// in use is reused. 'present' ends it: a submission that waits for the image
// to be acquired, if no submission did yet, signals the image's
// render-finished semaphore and the frame's fence, and the image is
// presented once the semaphore is. One render-finished semaphore per
// swapchain image, not per frame, is what the Vulkan guide recommends, since
// presentation holds it longer than a frame. A program with no swapchain
// ends each frame with 'device.endFrame()'.
//
// A swapchain that is out of date or suboptimal (the window was resized) is
// made again, after the device is idle; so is one after 'resize'. A window
// with no area (minimized) gives no texture: its status is SKIPPED.
//
// Threads. Command recording is why this layer exists instead of WebGPU's
// own: Vulkan, Direct3D 12 and Metal record commands on many threads at once,
// each into buffers from its own command pool, and submit them together.
// Cone has no threads yet, so there is one recording thread now; the design
// is Vulkan's convention for more: each frame in flight holds one command
// pool per recording thread (FrameState's pool is thread 0's), a thread
// records only from its own pool of the current frame, so pools are never
// shared and need no lock, and the Queue, which submits, belongs to one
// thread. When threads come, 'createCommandEncoder' takes the thread's index
// and each frame holds an array of pools; the objects' shapes do not change.
//
// Synchronization. Barriers are this layer's, not the caller's, as in WebGPU.
// Each texture has a layout it rests in between commands: a swapchain's the
// one presentation wants, one the device made to draw into the attachment
// layout of its format, one shaders sample the shader-read layout (it is
// moved there when it is made, and back there after 'queue.writeTexture'
// copies into it). A render pass moves a
// swapchain texture to the attachment layout and back, and puts a barrier
// before each attachment for what earlier commands did with it; a copy from
// a texture moves it to the copy-source layout and back. An attachment
// cleared at the start of a pass is moved from 'undefined', since what it
// held is not kept. 'queue.writeBuffer' copies from staging memory, in
// commands submitted ahead of the next submission, between a barrier after
// everything earlier (a write after a read) and one before everything later
// (a read after the write). Vulkan 1.3's synchronization2 barriers and
// dynamic rendering (no render-pass objects) are used throughout. The
// barriers are checked by the Khronos validation layer's synchronization
// validation, which the instance turns on with the layer (below).
//
// Memory. Every buffer and texture has a memory allocation of its own (a
// dedicated allocation): a buffer the CPU maps (MAP_READ or MAP_WRITE) in
// memory the CPU sees and keeps mapped, anything else in the GPU's own
// memory, filled by 'queue.writeBuffer' through staging memory. Staging is a
// block per frame in flight, handed out from its start (a bump allocator)
// and reset when the frame comes round; a write too big for the block
// retires it until then and takes a bigger one. Suballocating buffers and
// textures from larger blocks, as the Vulkan Memory Allocator (VMA) does, is
// the refinement for when there are hundreds of them: Vulkan guarantees only
// 4096 allocations.
//
// Lifetimes. An object's finalizer destroys it once the device is idle, so
// one dropped while a frame in flight may still use it is safe; this waits,
// which dropping objects in a frame loop would feel. A buffer given to
// 'queue.releaseBuffer' (WebGPU's 'buffer.destroy()') is instead retired to
// the frame in flight, and destroyed when that frame comes round again, its
// fence waited on: the deletion queue, for replacing what is drawn frame by
// frame without a stall. The other objects' retiring the same way is the
// refinement.
//
// Errors. Every Vulkan call that answers a VkResult is checked; a failure is
// written to stderr with the result's name ("gpu: vkQueuePresentKHR failed:
// ERROR_DEVICE_LOST") and counted, and 'failures()' answers how many. When
// the Khronos validation layer (VK_LAYER_KHRONOS_validation, from the Vulkan
// SDK) is installed, the instance enables it, with its synchronization
// validation where the layer takes settings (VK_EXT_layer_settings); and when
// the loader offers VK_EXT_debug_utils, the layer's warnings and errors go to
// stderr and are counted by 'validationMessages()'. Without them, they are
// off, and say so.
//
// Shaders are SPIR-V, compiled ahead of time from Slang by
// tools/shaders/shaders.py and embedded in the Cone source that uses them as
// arrays of 32-bit words; that tool's header is the guide.
//
// Its files are one module, and only this one, the root, imports:
//
//   constants.cone  the constants: formats, usages, modes, limits, statuses
//   failures.cone   failures and validation messages counted, small helpers,
//                   the layer and extension queries
//   instance.cone   Instance, Surface and Adapter
//   queue.cone      the frames in flight, and the Queue
//   device.cone     Device, the end of a frame, and memory allocation
//   resources.cone  formats translated to Vulkan's, Buffer, Texture,
//                   TextureView and SurfaceTexture
//   pipeline.cone   ShaderModule, Sampler, BindGroupLayout, PipelineLayout,
//                   RenderPipeline and BindGroup
//   swapchain.cone  SwapChain
//   commands.cone   barriers, CommandEncoder, RenderPass and CommandBuffer

mod gpu;

import libc;
import sdl;
import vulkan;
import geomath use Color;
import collections use String;
